  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   The current parent solution from Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem has been improved by C=0.3808. I propose a strategy to further reduce this bound by focusing on step functions with higher frequency oscillations. The insight is that dense high-frequency patterns may have closer interactions with their shifts while still maintaining a small maximum overlap. This will involve:\n   - Using stochastic gradient-based optimization with multiple restarts\n   - Employing a wavelet-inspired sparse initialization\n   - Applying dimensionality reduction via Fourier analysis to track important frequencies\n   - Incorporating periodic symmetry to leverage self-similarity\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   - Step function representation: Piecewise constant function on [0,2] sampled at `n_points`\n   - Objective: Convolution over [-(n-1),n-1] lags followed by max and scaling by dx\n   - Constraints: Integral=1, [0,1] values\n   - Fourier domain consideration: Identify onset of oscillations using wavelet coefficients\n   - Parent transfer: Use linear interpolation from initial points\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   ```python\n   1. Parent handling:\n      - If parent available, sample points with linear interpolation\n      - Create multiple random starts for diversity\n    \n   2. Initialization:\n      - Wavelet-based initialization: Random selection of high-frequency components\n      - Projection to [0,1] interval with fixed integral constraint\n      - Sum constraint: project_onto_box_sum(target=n/2.0)\n    \n   3. Optimization:\n      - Define objective function with gradient estimation\n      - Use stochastic gradient descent with adaptive learning rate\n      - Apply exponential averaging of gradients\n      - Implement periodic restarts with increasing frequency\n    \n   4. Hyperparameters:\n      - Initial learning rate: 0.1 to 0.05 based on gradient scaling\n      - Epoch length: 100 operations per point\n      - Random seed heuristics: 4 seeds for different amplitude profiles\n    \n   5. Validation:\n      - After optimization, verify integral preservation\n      - Snap to grid if necessary to maintain step function property\n      - Compare scores against known bounds\n    \n   6. Upsampling:\n      - After budget check, upsample by factor of 2 using spectrum preservation\n      - Project back to valid constraints with minimal score increase\n   ```\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n   - Main schedule: 3 \u00d7 1000 operations per restart\n   - Parameter ranges:\n     - Step size: 0.01 to 0.1 adaptively\n     - Restart frequency: 10 restarts at worst\n     - Wavelet families: Haar only\n   - Key variation: Disabling gradient clipping temporarily during startup\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   - Main objective: Complete within 1100s with:\n     - 30 updates per function call\n     - 5 optimization starts\n     - 1 upsample attempt\n   - Budget splits:\n     - 30% for validation projection\n     - 50% for gradient descent\n     - 20% for evaluation and upsample\n   - Stability measures:\n     - Track best score over time\n     - Automatically resume at 95% time if stuck\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   - Final score calculation using:\n     1. np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")\n     2. Multiply by dx=2.0/n\n     3. Track maximum correlation value\n   - Return format: (h.tolist(), float(c5_val), int(n))\n   - Must preserve:\n     - [0,1] values\n     - Integral=1\n     - Finite returns\n   - Graceful degradation for out-of-bound values",
